## Corrections to the primary analysis

Its one-line fixture repair is sound, but calling the cause simply "the product"
and blaming an unprescribed assertion overstates the evidence. The accepted
assertion tested legitimate storage-failure behavior before the new guard.
Adding meaningful assertions within an authorized fact is not inherently an
agent defect. The present gap is that later behavior and the actual accepted
test fixture need migration together, but the later step names only one of the
affected fixtures. The current production implementation need not be weakened.

The primary's instruction not to edit `.specrc` during execution is obsolete
and contradicts the operator's explicit ruling. ASG 0090 manual 033 permits
valid live configuration changes and all `.manual/` descendants. Its statement
that a project-prompt edit caused 0160-03 is also wrong: that incident involved
`.specrc.agent`. Prompt files remain a distinct publication input.

Its pending-terminal-event statement was true while the report was being
written; the terminal event now exists and the monitor handled it correctly.
The pending gate table also does not erase the actual successful build evidence.

No isolated two-file commit is required. The operator may commit all escalation
work, as usual. I do not recommend the primary's accept-current alternative:
normal review and measurement should publish checkpoint 184 before step 0186.

