## Recurrence and prevention

The recurring class is an accepted fixture affected by later behavior but omitted
from that later step's edit authority. A retry cannot add that authority. The
existing bounded escalation correctly avoids silently changing tests.

I recommend the specific amendment above. A general permission to change any
fixture merely while retaining assertion text is too broad: fixture changes can
remove the behavior an assertion actually exercises. If the operator wants a
standing rule, it should require the same named obligation, preserved identities
and assertions, explicit coverage of both the old failure path and the newly
introduced guard, and judge review of the semantic effect. That would be a new
operator policy, not authority supplied by this report or a reason to suppress
the present gate. No backend-specific retry or budget increase is justified.
